{"passages": [{"reference": "5.1", "verdict": "accept", "checks": {"complete_coverage": true, "no_added_claims": true, "names_and_negations": true, "lexical_fidelity": true, "uncertainties_disclosed": true}, "findings": []}, {"reference": "5.2", "verdict": "accept", "checks": {"complete_coverage": true, "no_added_claims": true, "names_and_negations": true, "lexical_fidelity": true, "uncertainties_disclosed": true}, "findings": []}, {"reference": "5.3", "verdict": "revise", "checks": {"complete_coverage": false, "no_added_claims": false, "names_and_negations": true, "lexical_fidelity": false, "uncertainties_disclosed": false}, "findings": [{"source_quote": "ὁ δὲ ἐρᾶν με γυναικὸς ἀληθῶς ἀναπεισθεὶς ἔφη", "english_quote": "Fully convinced that I truly loved a woman", "issue": "The draft supplies both complete conviction through “Fully” and genuine love through “truly.” The Greek supplies one ἀληθῶς, which can qualify either the loving or the conviction; ἀναπεισθείς does not independently establish the added degree of completeness. This attachment uncertainty is not discussed in the notes.", "suggested_revision": "Use “Convinced that I was truly in love with a woman” without “Fully.” Add a note preserving the alternative “truly convinced that I was in love with a woman,” explaining that the main rendering attaches ἀληθῶς to the supposed love because Appion is interpreting Clement’s affected behavior.", "severity": "minor"}, {"source_quote": "καὶ ἅπαξ ἐπιτυχών, ἐπαυσάμην τοῦ ἔρωτος.", "english_quote": "once I had succeeded, I stopped being in love.", "issue": "Here “once” functions as an English temporal conjunction, meaning “after” or “when.” It does not explicitly preserve the numerical force of ἅπαξ: Appion succeeded once and then ceased being in love.", "suggested_revision": "Replace this wording with “after I had succeeded once, I stopped being in love,” or another rendering that explicitly preserves a single success. The preceding interpretation of πεισθεὶς and its modifiers is defensible and need not be replaced merely because its disclosed alternative remains possible.", "severity": "major"}]}, {"reference": "5.4", "verdict": "accept", "checks": {"complete_coverage": true, "no_added_claims": true, "names_and_negations": true, "lexical_fidelity": true, "uncertainties_disclosed": true}, "findings": []}, {"reference": "5.5", "verdict": "revise", "checks": {"complete_coverage": true, "no_added_claims": true, "names_and_negations": true, "lexical_fidelity": false, "uncertainties_disclosed": true}, "findings": [{"source_quote": "Ταῦτα ἐμοὶ σοῦ μᾶλλον συγχώρει εἰδέναι.", "english_quote": "Allow me to know these matters better than you do.", "issue": "In modern English, “Allow me to know” reads as a request for permission rather than a demand to concede Appion’s existing superior knowledge. The lexical note correctly identifies the concessive sense of συγχώρει, but the main wording does not clearly express it.", "suggested_revision": "Use “Grant that I know these matters better than you do” or “Concede that I know more about these matters than you do.”", "severity": "minor"}]}]}
